WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:06.250
I began moving them around and I wanted an arrangement,

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.410
you know, where I had a big and a small molecule.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.850
So how did you do it?

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.120
Somehow you had to form link bonds.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.210
So here's A and here's T 

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.090
and I wanted this hydrogen to point directly at this nitrogen.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.570
So I had something like this.

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:37.060
So then I went to the pair and wanted this nitrogen to point to this one,

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:38.540
I went like this.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.050
They look the same.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:46.150
You can put one right on top of the other.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:00.150
We knew,

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:04.160
even if we go up to the ceiling, we were building a tiny fraction 

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.600
of a molecule. A hundred million of these  base pairs in one 

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:18.790
molecule, all fitting into this wonderful symmetry which we saw the 

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.570
morning of February 28th, 1953.

